# Copy to .env and adjust values as needed.
#
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#  DEVELOPMENT TEMPLATE — THE CREDENTIALS BELOW ARE DELIBERATELY WEAK
#
#  This file feeds the base docker-compose.yml, which is tuned for a laptop and binds every
#  published port to 127.0.0.1. The database passwords here are throwaway placeholders that
#  are published in this repository — they are dev-scoped, not secrets, and they are listed
#  as out of scope in SECURITY.md for exactly that reason.
#
#  Do not deploy from this file. Copy .env.prod.example instead: it ships every secret
#  empty on purpose, and the deploy refuses to start until you fill them in rather than
#  falling back to a value published here. See docs/deployment.md.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# API runtime
# Use Development to enable CORS and Swagger in the containerized dev experience.
ASPNETCORE_ENVIRONMENT=Development
SWAGGER_ENABLED=true
USE_IN_MEMORY_DATABASE=false

# Seed the deterministic demo dataset (accounts, budgets, transactions + four demo
# login users). Development/Testing only: the migration service seeds in no other environment,
# and setting this to true elsewhere does nothing but log a warning.
# Default true for the dev stack — set false to start with an empty database.
SEED_DEMO_DATA=true

# MariaDB credentials/databases. Weak by design and dev-scoped — see the banner at the top of
# this file. docker-compose.yml falls back to these same values when .env is absent, so they are
# the published dev defaults, not a suggestion to reuse anywhere reachable from a network.
MARIADB_ROOT_PASSWORD=root_password
MARIADB_USER=odyssey
MARIADB_PASSWORD=odyssey_password

# One EF context, one database, named "odyssey" by default. It cannot be split: identity,
# finance and journal are a single model with real foreign keys between them, so ODYSSEY_DATABASE
# names the whole thing. (The container only auto-creates MARIADB_DATABASE, so keep the two
# names in step unless you create the second database yourself.)
MARIADB_DATABASE=odyssey
ODYSSEY_DATABASE=odyssey

# ── Bootstrap administrator (optional locally) ───────────────────────────────
# The demo seed (SEED_DEMO_DATA=true) already creates an Admin — admin@demo.example.com
# with the shared demo password — so these are unnecessary for the dev stack. Set them if you
# run with SEED_DEMO_DATA=false against an empty database, otherwise the migrations job fails.
# Applied only when the database has no users; on an already-seeded volume they do nothing.
#BOOTSTRAP_ADMIN_EMAIL=admin@odyssey.local
#BOOTSTRAP_ADMIN_PASSWORD=

# Registration approval and email-confirmation policy are no longer static config (issue #349) —
# they're admin-editable at runtime via the System Settings page / PUT /api/system-settings, backed
# by the database. Both default on for a fresh database (see the AddSystemSettings migration seed).

# LEGAL_PSEUDONYMIZATION_SECRET is gone (issue #445 Wave 4). The salt that pseudonymises personal
# data in retained legal/audit records moved into the encrypted secret store and is entered at
# System Settings -> Credentials. Nothing needs setting locally: outside Production an unset one
# falls back to the same fixed, deliberately obvious development value it always did.

# Transactional email (account confirmation / password reset).
# There is NO email configuration left in this file (issue #8). Every part of it is now edited at
# System Settings → Email, backed by the database, and takes effect on the next send with no restart:
#
#   the relay host, port and STARTTLS mode, and the public origin links point at  (issue #8)
#   the sender identity and the per-recipient send throttle                       (issue #421)
#   the relay username and password, in the encrypted secret store                (issue #445)
#
# Nothing needs setting for local development. An unset SMTP host means "mail is not configured":
# every send is logged and skipped, and in Development/Testing the action link is logged in full so
# confirmation and password reset still work with no mail server. No other environment logs it — a
# password-reset link is a working credential.
#
# The dev stack seeds the client base URL to http://localhost:5199 itself, so reset links work out of
# the box.

# AI file analysis (Claude). Extracts transactions from uploaded statements/receipts and
# suggests counterparty/tag matches. Off by default — set true and supply an Anthropic API key.
# The key is a secret: keep it in your local .env only (.env* is gitignored), never commit it.
# The processor disclosure shown in the consent gate (processor, region, lawful basis, privacy
# notice URL), the future-dated-transaction window and the auto-link confidence threshold are no
# longer static config (issue #421) — they're admin-editable at runtime via System Settings →
# File analysis, backed by the database.
#
# FILE_ANALYSIS_API_KEY is gone (issue #445 Wave 1): the key moved into the encrypted secret store and
# is entered at System Settings -> Credentials. Until it is, analysis requests go out without a key and
# each job is recorded as failed. Only the PROVIDER is still deploy-time config — a persisted enum with
# one member.

# The switch, the model and the destination followed them into System Settings → File analysis in
# issue #439, as did the tuning values (max tokens, match vocabulary, match timeout) in issue #434.
# None of them has an environment variable: turn analysis on in the UI, where the shipped defaults
# are analysis OFF, claude-sonnet-5 and https://api.anthropic.com.
#
# Note what turning analysis on means: the switch is honoured on the NEXT request rather than up to
# 30 seconds later, and the API key is sent to whatever base URL is in force — so repoint that only
# to a host you control or trust. It must be an absolute https:// address with no path; the provider
# appends /v1/messages itself.

# Upload size. FILE_UPLOAD_MAX_BYTES is the TRANSPORT ceiling only — the Kestrel request-body and
# multipart limits fixed at startup. The cap a user is actually validated against is admin-editable at
# runtime in System Settings → Storage (issue #421), and can be lowered freely but never raised above
# this number, because the transport would reject the request before the setting was consulted. Raise
# this (and the reverse proxy's body-size limit — see docs/deployment.md) before raising the setting.
# It is read by the API at startup; the admin-editable cap is set in the UI and seeds at 64 MB.
FILE_UPLOAD_MAX_BYTES=67108864

# Identity rate limit: requests per minute per client IP across the whole MapIdentityApi group
# (/login, /register, the two mail routes, and /manage/*). The value shipped in appsettings.json —
# and the one PRODUCTION runs, since docker-compose.prod.yml resets this key back out — is 30.
#
# This dev stack raises it because the E2E suites drive one stack from one address, so they share a
# single rate-limit partition. A browser test costs many permits rather than the one its login
# suggests: the Blazor client calls GET manage/info to resolve auth state on sign-in and on
# navigation. Odyssey.E2ETests alone spends a 30-permit window; Odyssey.E2ETests.Api another six.
#
# Lower it to 30 to reproduce production's limiting locally — expect the E2E suites to fail with
# "Login failed: RateLimited" and login navigation timeouts if you do while running them.
IDENTITY_RATE_LIMIT=300
